Historian Lena Ortiz argues that the spread of the telegraph in the 1840s made American newspaper reporting more uniform. Because wire copy could be sent to many papers at once, editors increasingly published the same national and international dispatches, Ortiz contends, gradually eroding distinctive local reporting styles.

